WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.643
A function gives exactly one output for each allowed input

00:00:04.643 --> 00:00:10.584
Squaring is a function even though two different inputs can produce the same output

00:00:10.584 --> 00:00:16.100
Negative two and positive two both give four, without violating the one-output requirement.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.653
The domain lists allowed inputs, while the range lists outputs actually reached

00:00:22.653 --> 00:00:29.218
For the real squaring function, every real input is allowed, but no negative output occurs

00:00:29.218 --> 00:00:34.033
Reading the horizontal and vertical coverage keeps these two ideas distinct.

00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:38.174
A formula can restrict its own domain

00:00:38.174 --> 00:00:42.638
The reciprocal rule excludes input two because its denominator would vanish

00:00:42.638 --> 00:00:51.567
The two branches belong to one function, but there is no output at the excluded input, regardless of the visible graph window.

00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:56.989
Not every relation defines y as a function of x

00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:02.761
In this sideways parabola, input four is paired with both two and negative two

00:01:02.761 --> 00:01:08.533
A vertical line meets the relation twice, exposing the failure of the one-output condition.
